def _guide_sec_save(n, accent, ink):
    """§ Save & export."""
    _li = ("font-size:12.5pt; line-height:150%; margin:3px 0;")
    html = f"""
<h2 style="color:{accent}; font-size:16.5pt;">{n}&nbsp;&nbsp;Save &amp; export</h2>
<p style="{_GP}">Your work is a <b><code>.zirclock</code></b> project file.
Save it with <b>Save</b> in the side rail, <b>File → Save</b> or Ctrl/⌘+S;
<b>Save As</b> writes a copy under a new name. The window title shows the file of
the project on screen, and a <b>*</b> there (with a <b>dot on the Save button</b>)
means that project has unsaved changes. With several projects open, each keeps its
own mark; when you quit, ZirClock lists the ones with changes and <b>Save all</b>
saves each one in its file — or the workspace, if you opened one. The file stores
your samples, data, sets and settings — <i>not</i> the computed tables, which
recompute the moment you open the project.</p>
<p style="{_GP}">Bringing data in and taking results out:</p>
<p style="{_li}"><b><span style="color:{accent};">Import</span></b> — read data from
a spreadsheet (header row found automatically; stacked samples split; same-name
samples can be merged).</p>
<p style="{_li}"><b><span style="color:{accent};">Export → Export Table 2</span></b>
— the full results table at full precision (data, weighted means, Ti, Hf).</p>
<p style="{_li}"><b><span style="color:{accent};">Export → Export current graph</span></b>
— the diagram on screen, as PDF, SVG, PNG, TIFF or EPS. For a figure you will
publish, choose PDF or SVG: they are vector, and EPS cannot carry transparency.</p>
<p style="{_li}"><b><span style="color:{accent};">Export → Methods &amp; references</span></b>
— a draft methods paragraph tailored to your project, with its citations.</p>
<p style="{_li}"><b><span style="color:{accent};">Figures</span></b> — or assemble
several diagrams in the compositor and export the whole page.</p>
<p style="{_GP}">The <b>Export</b> button of the side rail asks which of the three
exports you want, so you do not have to remember where each one lives. Under
<b>Help</b> you will find the <b>Theory &amp; methods</b> compendium (the
mathematics behind every calculation) and this guide.</p>
<p style="{_GP}">On macOS, closing the window leaves ZirClock running in the Dock,
as any other Mac application: click its icon to bring your work back. Use
<b>Quit</b> (⌘Q) to close it for good — that is when it asks about unsaved
changes.</p>
<p style="{_GP}">That is the whole loop: set up a project, choose your variables,
enter data, read the results, visualise, and export. Happy dating!</p>
"""
    return html, {}


